---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '@components/Figure.astro';
import ShowFigure from '@components/ShowFigure.astro';
import { resolvePath } from '@lib/paths';

// The project story, in the order the work happened. Numbers come from the
// ORLO-Zig simulation repo (deliverables/zo24, zo25, zo26) and the pod
// clinic briefing (pod-clinic/issue_body.md).

const sections = [
  { id: 'problem', label: 'The problem' },
  { id: 'robots', label: 'One robot or two' },
  { id: 'paths', label: 'Shapes and paths' },
  { id: 'spiral', label: 'The outward spiral' },
  { id: 'wheel', label: 'Reaction wheel sizing' },
  { id: 'two-robot', label: 'Two mirrored robots' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What is not yet known' },
  { id: 'next', label: 'Next steps' },
  { id: 'literature', label: 'Side project: literature toolkit' },
];

const pathTable = [
  { path: 'Snake (baseline)', time: '303.8', peak: '61.7', final: '61.7' },
  { path: 'Spiral outward (selected)', time: '309.1', peak: '~180, not resolved', final: '46.4', pick: true },
  { path: 'Spiral inward', time: '308.6', peak: '~180, failed sampling check', final: '29.2' },
];

const wheelTable = [
  { set: 'No wheel limit (ideal)', time: '5.15', mass: 'none' },
  { set: 'BCT RW4-HT ×3 (recommended)', time: '13.5', mass: '9.6', pick: true },
  { set: 'BCT RW4 ×3', time: '18.6', mass: '9.6' },
  { set: 'BCT RW8 ×3', time: '18.6', mass: '13.2' },
  { set: 'BCT RW1 ×3', time: '37.1', mass: '3.3' },
  { set: 'NanoAvionics 4RW0', time: '290.4', mass: '0.7' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Project"
  description="How the assembly strategy for a 10x10 tile structure in space was chosen, and how a reaction wheel was sized to hold its attitude."
>
  <section class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-gradient-to-b from-arc-50/60 to-white">
    <div class="container-page pt-14 pb-12">
      <p class="eyebrow">Project · MIT 16.S893</p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 max-w-3xl">Assembling a tile structure in space without tumbling it</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-lg text-slate-600">
        A robot that builds a structure in free space pushes on that structure with every move. With
        no ground to push against, the structure turns. This project simulates how the ORLO* assembly
        robots build a 10×10 grid of square tiles, each about 160 mm on a side. It then picks a build order and sizes a reaction wheel
        that holds the structure still.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid max-w-3xl grid-cols-2 gap-6 sm:grid-cols-4">
        <div><p class="stat m-0">100</p><p class="m-0 text-sm text-slate-500">tiles, 160 mm each</p></div>
        <div><p class="stat m-0">6</p><p class="m-0 text-sm text-slate-500">layouts compared</p></div>
        <div><p class="stat m-0">46.4°</p><p class="m-0 text-sm text-slate-500">final tilt, no wheel</p></div>
        <div><p class="stat m-0">13.5 min</p><p class="m-0 text-sm text-slate-500">robot motion with a wheel</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="container-page grid gap-12 pt-12 lg:grid-cols-[12rem_1fr]">
    <aside class="hidden lg:block">
      <nav class="sticky top-24 text-sm">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3 text-slate-400">On this page</p>
        <ol class="m-0 list-none space-y-1 p-0">
          {sections.map((s, i) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`#${s.id}`} class="block rounded px-2 py-1 text-slate-500 no-underline hover:bg-slate-50 hover:text-arc-700">
                {s.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </nav>
    </aside>

    <article class="article max-w-3xl">
      <Figure
        number={1}
        src="/media/project-flow.svg"
        alt="Flow chart: problem, robot count, path search, robustness, decision on the outward spiral, wheel sizing; then next steps on a mirrored two-robot team and failure recovery; the literature toolkit feeds all steps."
        caption="The research path. Each step narrowed the design. The literature toolkit runs beside the main track and feeds test methods and prior work into each step."
      />

      <!-- 1 -->
      <section id="problem" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2>1. The problem</h2>
        <p>
          ORLO* (Orbital Locker Surface Tile Assembly Robot) builds a flat grid from square tiles, each about 160 mm on a side. The
          study starts with a 10×10 grid in free space. Nothing holds the structure, so its total
          angular momentum stays zero. When a robot and its tile move one way, the structure turns
          the other way.
        </p>
        <p>
          The questions are simple. How long does the build take? How far does the structure turn
          during the build, and at the end? The simulation tracks every moving body: robots, gantry
          and tiles. Every number on this page comes from that simulation.
        </p>
        <ShowFigure from="controls" id="assembly-3d" number={2} />
      </section>

      <!-- 2 -->
      <section id="robots" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <h2>2. One robot or two</h2>
        <p>
          The original ORLO* design uses two robots. R1 stores the tiles and moves along the Y axis.
          R2 carries 10 tiles at a time along the X axis, places them, and returns to R1 to reload.
          This is strategy <strong>A1</strong>. Strategy <strong>A2</strong> uses one robot that
          carries every tile along a single path and never reloads.
        </p>
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="a1-vs-a2" number={3} />
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="a1-vs-a2-results" number={4} />
        <div class="card my-6 grid grid-cols-1 divide-y divide-slate-200 sm:grid-cols-2 sm:divide-x sm:divide-y-0">
          <div class="p-5">
            <p class="eyebrow text-slate-500">A1 · two robots</p>
            <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-sm">1086 s motion · peak tilt 24.4° · final tilt 14.0°</p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5">
            <p class="eyebrow">A2 · one robot</p>
            <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-sm">304 s motion · peak and final tilt 61.7°</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>
          A2 was faster and simpler, so the next studies used one robot. The two-robot idea comes back
          later in a new form. In that form, two robots move in opposite directions so that their
          effects cancel.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 3 -->
      <section id="paths" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <h2>3. Shapes and paths for one robot</h2>
        <p>
          The build order sets how far the structure turns. I tested six layouts with the same
          assumptions. Three were 10×10 squares: snake, outward spiral and inward spiral. Three were
          free 100-tile shapes: straight, staircase and elbow. A seeded search found two more square
          routes, but the snake beat both of them.
        </p>
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="square-paths" number={5} />
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="square-tradeoff" number={6} />
        <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-300 text-left text-slate-500">
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Path (10×10)</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-right font-medium">Motion time (s)</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-right font-medium">Peak tilt (°)</th>
                <th class="py-2 text-right font-medium">Final tilt (°)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {pathTable.map((r) => (
                <tr class:list={['border-b border-slate-200', r.pick && 'bg-arc-50 font-medium text-slate-900']}>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4">{r.path}</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right font-mono">{r.time}</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right font-mono">{r.peak}</td>
                  <td class="py-2 text-right font-mono">{r.final}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          I also changed the mass and inertia values in 20 robustness runs. 12 runs passed, 8 gave no
          clear result, and none failed. The snake passed all 10 of its runs, with a peak tilt between 56° and 78°.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 4 -->
      <section id="spiral" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <h2>4. The decision: build in an outward spiral</h2>
        <p>
          The snake has a lower peak tilt and is about 5 s faster. I chose the outward spiral because
          of how each new tile joins the structure. From the fourth tile on, most new tiles join
          <strong>two</strong> tiles that are already in place. That gives a stronger joint.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both paths have 18 tiles with only one anchor. In the snake, the full first row of 10 tiles
          is a straight line with one anchor each. In the spiral, the single-anchor tiles are spread
          out. Each one starts a new side next to a compact block. The spiral also ends with less tilt
          (46.4° against 61.7°). A reaction wheel can take care of the high peak.
        </p>
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="spiral-outward-stages" number={7} />
      </section>

      <!-- 5 -->
      <section id="wheel" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <h2>5. Sizing a reaction wheel to hold the structure still</h2>
        <p>
          A reaction wheel stores momentum so that the structure does not have to. To hold the
          structure still, the wheels must store the opposite of the robot's momentum at every moment.
          The wheel motors must supply the matching torque:
        </p>
        <div class="card my-5 px-5 py-4 font-mono text-sm text-slate-800">
          h<sub>wheel</sub>(t) = −h<sub>robot</sub>(t)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;τ<sub>motor</sub>(t) = −dh<sub>robot</sub>/dt
        </div>
        <p>
          At full robot speed, the spiral needs a peak of <strong>7.33 N·m·s</strong> of momentum and
          <strong>18.0 N·m</strong> of torque on the z axis. No flown wheel has that much torque. The
          best set has 0.50 N·m, which is about 36 times too small. The fix is to slow each robot move
          until the wheel can keep up.
        </p>
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="reaction-wheel-results" number={8} />
        <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-300 text-left text-slate-500">
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Wheel set</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-right font-medium">Robot motion (min)</th>
                <th class="py-2 text-right font-medium">Wheel mass (kg)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {wheelTable.map((r) => (
                <tr class:list={['border-b border-slate-200', r.pick && 'bg-arc-50 font-medium text-slate-900']}>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4">{r.set}</td>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right font-mono">{r.time}</td>
                  <td class="py-2 text-right font-mono">{r.mass}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Torque, not momentum, limits the build. The RW8 has more momentum than the RW4, but they give
          the same time because both have 0.25 N·m of torque. I chose three BCT RW4-HT wheels. A
          flight-style controller with a noisy gyro and star tracker held the attitude within
          <strong>0.01°</strong> in simulation.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a class="btn-secondary" href={resolvePath('/show/zig/walkthroughs/reaction-wheel-sizing-walkthrough.html')} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sizing walkthrough ↗</a>
          <a class="btn-secondary" href={resolvePath('/show/zig/docs/Reaction_Wheel_Comparison.pdf')} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wheel comparison (PDF) ↗</a>
          <a class="btn-secondary" href={resolvePath('/show/zig/docs/Reaction_Wheel_Sizing_Equations.pdf')} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sizing equations (PDF) ↗</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 6 -->
      <section id="two-robot" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <h2>6. Two mirrored robots to remove the yaw wheel</h2>
        <p>
          Next I tested two robots that are mirror images of each other. Each robot builds one half of
          the 10×10 grid and carries 49 tiles. The two seed tiles sit side by side at the centre line.
          Each half uses a U-cutback path: nested U shapes that grow out from the centre line.
        </p>
        <p>
          A mirror reverses the turning push of each robot about two of the three axes. When the robots
          move in step, their yaw (z) and pitch (y) momentum cancel to zero. Roll (x) does not cancel.
          Both robots drive the same way along the rows and lift tiles at the same row, so their roll
          pushes add. With no wheels at all, the structure rolls up to 16° during the build and ends
          within 0.5° of where it started.
        </p>
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="two-robot-no-wheels" number={9} />
        <p>
          With a wheel on roll (and none on yaw), the structure stays still. One flown BCT RW4-HT wheel
          (3.2 kg) on roll is enough. Robot motion then takes 3.3 min, against 13.5 min for one robot
          with three of these wheels. Turning one path 180° instead of mirroring it makes yaw four
          times worse.
        </p>
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="two-robot-mirror" number={10} />
        <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-300 text-left text-slate-500">
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Case</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-4 text-right font-medium">Wheel momentum x / y / z (N·m·s)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">One robot, outward spiral</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right font-mono">1.09 / 1.14 / 7.33</td></tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-arc-50 font-medium text-slate-900"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Mirrored pair, in step</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right font-mono">1.13 / 0 / 0</td></tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Pair, one path turned 180°</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right font-mono">0 / 0 / 31.1</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The catch is timing. If robot B is late, the push from robot A has no partner for that time.
          The yaw grows by about 0.5° for each 10 ms of delay. To keep yaw below 1°, the robots must
          stay within about <strong>0.02 s</strong> of each other. That needs a shared clock and the
          same motion plan.
        </p>
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="two-robot-timing" number={11} />
        <p>
          Each robot here has a placeholder dry mass of 56.7 kg, which is two thirds of the single
          robot, because each robot carries half the tiles. I will re-run these results when the new
          tile and robot masses are set. One mirror pair cannot cancel all three axes. Four robots, one
          in each quadrant and mirrored across both centre lines, could cancel roll too. I have not
          simulated that yet.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 7 -->
      <section id="limits" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <h2>7. What is not yet known</h2>
        <p>
          The model treats every body as rigid and isolated. It has no gravity gradient, no joint loads
          and no flexible modes. The motor limits are not checked against real hardware, and the
          wheel mass is not yet added to the structure. The map below shows what is checked, what is
          assumed and what is still unknown.
        </p>
        <ShowFigure from="zig" id="validation-gap-map" number={12} />
        <p>
          Joining the seams sets the real finish time. If each seam takes 600 s, joining takes about
          30 hours, and robot motion is less than 0.3 % of the total.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 8 -->
      <section id="next" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <h2>8. Next steps</h2>
        <ol class="space-y-3 pl-5">
          <li>
            <strong>New parameters.</strong> The tiles may become plastic, and the geometry and robot
            masses have changed. I will update the inputs and re-run the wheel and two-robot results.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Failure recovery.</strong> If one robot fails, the other robot must detect the
            failure, find the state of the build, and finish. I plan to test this with simple
            simulations and with test methods from the literature.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Path count.</strong> Count and score all build orders on the MIT ORCD Engaging
            cluster.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- Side project -->
      <section id="literature" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-6 md:p-8">
          <p class="eyebrow">Side project</p>
          <h2 class="mt-1">A literature review toolkit</h2>
          <p>
            This tool is not a step in the main track. It supports my research workflow. Zotero holds
            the PDFs, and a spreadsheet holds the notes. A Better BibTeX citekey joins the two. Papers
            are sorted into four stages: value, method, improvement and robustness. Each paper also
            gets a read level from 0 (skip) to 3 (study).
          </p>
          <div class="my-6 grid grid-cols-3 gap-4">
            <div><p class="stat m-0">235</p><p class="m-0 text-sm text-slate-500">papers logged</p></div>
            <div><p class="stat m-0">332</p><p class="m-0 text-sm text-slate-500">search queries logged</p></div>
            <div><p class="stat m-0">9</p><p class="m-0 text-sm text-slate-500">paper cards, 140 quotes checked</p></div>
          </div>
          <ShowFigure from="zig" id="literature-pipeline" number={13} />
          <p class="mb-0">
            Every paper must have a DOI or another stable ID, so nothing is cited from memory. Every
            takeaway on a card has a page number and an exact quote. A script checks each quote
            against the PDF text.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-6">
            <a class="btn-primary" href="https://github.com/michaelkuhl10/lit-review-toolkit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Use the toolkit (GitHub) ↗</a>
            <a class="btn-secondary" href={resolvePath('/show/zig/walkthroughs/literature-toolkit-walkthrough.html')} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Literature toolkit walkthrough ↗</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <p class="mt-16 border-t border-slate-200 pt-6 text-sm text-slate-500">
        This work started from the <a href={resolvePath('/docs/Kuhl_Project_Proposal.pdf')} target="_blank" rel="noopener">project proposal</a>
        (20 September 2026) on lightweight computer vision for ORLO*. The work moved to assembly strategy
        and attitude control first, because the vision task depends on them. Session details are in the
        <a href={resolvePath('/dev-log')}>dev log</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
